From c259d4696ef5ed5d0e3b4bdbe2e2ae71eeeda9fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AmirSalahY <hello@noury.ch>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 15:17:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/26] feat(ui): Amazon Q's action model for keys, and
 sortMethod for ordering
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Keys — named actions with per-key accept modes, adapted from Q's 34-action enum
to what a terminal can actually express:
  Alt+1..9    accept the Nth visible row      Alt+i  insert the common prefix
  Alt+Enter   accept and run                  Alt+d  toggle the description
  Alt+l / s   grow / shrink the list
Only Alt+letter, Alt+digit and Alt+Enter are bindable: terminals send Alt as an
ESC prefix, readline reports Alt+punctuation with no key name, and Alt+[ is
indistinguishable from the start of a CSI sequence. Verified before choosing
defaults. The three accept keys gain Q's modes (insert | insertOrPrefix |
ignore), replacing two booleans that could only express insert-or-nothing;
acceptOnEnter/acceptOnRightArrow still work as shorthands.

Ordering — sortMethod: frecency (default) | recency | alphabetical | none.
recency is a faithful port of Q's updatePriorities, limitations included, since
the point of offering it is to be that model. Both usage modes now also learn
from accepted suggestions (Q's updateAutocompleteIndexFromUserInsert), weighted
half an execution: a completion can be accepted and then thought better of.

Fixes two bugs found while building this:

1. Frecency was dead for every subcommand. rankSuggestions keyed off
   acceptedTokens, which the recursion seeds as [] and never fills with the
   command name, so it looked up "rebase" while history had stored
   "git rebase". Now keyed off the complete tokens in allTokens. Unit tests on
   rankingBoost() passed throughout; only an end-to-end test catches it, and
   there is now one.
2. alt+Enter reaches update() as name "return", so it tripped the
   clear-on-Enter branch and wiped the line executeSelected was about to
   complete.

Keys documented in Usage: inshellisense [options] [command]

IDE style command line auto complete

Options:
  -v, --version        output the current version
  -l, --login          start shell as a login shell
  -s, --shell <shell>  shell to use for command execution, supported shells:
                       bash, pwsh, zsh, fish, xonsh, nu
  -c, --check          check if shell is in an inshellisense session
  -V, --verbose        enable verbose logging
  -h, --help           display help for command

Commands:
  init [shell]         generates shell configurations & prints the source
                       command for a specified shell
  reinit               regenerates shell configurations
  doctor               checks the health of this inshellisense installation
  specs                manage specs
  complete <input>     generates a completion for the provided input
  uninstall            removes all cache resources and configuration for
                       inshellisense

Keys (while the suggestion menu is open):
  Tab                accept the highlighted suggestion
  Enter              accept, then Enter again to run
  Right              accept, only with the cursor at end of line
  Up / Down          move the selection
  Esc                dismiss for the rest of the line; Tab reopens
  Alt+1 .. Alt+9     accept the Nth visible suggestion
  Alt+i              insert the prefix common to every suggestion
  Alt+Enter          accept and run in one keystroke
  Alt+d              show/hide the description panel
  Alt+l / Alt+s      show more / fewer suggestions

Suggestion order (sortMethod):
  frecency           how often and how recently you ran it here (default)
  recency            last used only, no decay — Amazon Q's model
  alphabetical       by name, ignoring usage
  none               spec-declared priority only

Configure both in ~/.config/inshellisense/rc.toml, under [bindings], [keys] and
sortMethod. Alt+letter, Alt+digit and Alt+Enter are the only Alt combinations a
terminal reports reliably. and the README.
